exports[`lens prompts render as before > lens rust performance full based rich 1`] = `
"You are the **performance** review lens for a Rust diff. Review ONLY this slice; ignore everything else (other lenses cover it). Load the rust-review skill for the rubric and the rust-navigation skill for context expansion.

SLICE: performance: allocation in hot loops, to_string/to_owned where a borrow works, Vec::new+push where size is known, N+1 / repeated work in loops.

REVIEW RULES (all languages): read severity.md, classes.md, sweeps.md in the first existing dir (expand with Bash; never the reviewed repo's copy): \`/plugin/craft/skills/review-rules\`, \`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-rules\`, \`\${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/plugins/cache/craft/craft/9.9.9/skills/review-rules\`. None readable → say so; HARM below still holds.
HARM (every finding): \`harm\` = effect, who, recovery; \`harmLevel\` = severity.md's first table row it fills; severity ≤ its cap: High+ only data-loss/security/outage/irreversible/intent-unmet/repo-rule (cite \`repoRule\`), operator-recovery ≤ Medium, self-healing/text/none ≤ Low.

Diff base: \`origin/main\`. Review with \`git diff --merge-base 'origin/main' -- '*.rs'\`.


INTENT (what the change should do): add a retry
STATED SPEC / AUTHOR CLAIMS (verbatim PR/commit description — treat as the spec; the intent lens must check EACH claim against the code, and any lens may use it):
"""
Retries are idempotent.
Never fails on a missing child.
"""
HOT FILES (scrutinize harder): src/a.rs, src/b.rs
REPO RULES \`AGENTS.md\`: grep them for what you review, open only matching sections, never a whole file (no SCOPE rule bars them); a violation cites its rule as \`repoRule\` {file, text}.

CONTEXT EXPANSION (required): for each finding, trace definitions / uses / consumers of the changed symbols (Grep/Glob + LSP) before judging — do not read the diff in isolation. If a finding depends on code outside the diff, say so in \`why\`.
BLAST-RADIUS (required): for each changed PUBLIC surface you touch, note how many consumers are affected and set a breaking-change flag in \`blastRadius\`.
CONFIDENCE: report everything you suspect, located. Do NOT self-censor borderline findings — verification happens downstream; each still names its harm, and its severity is the one that harm allows. Each finding needs file:line (use file:"" line:0 only when truly not locatable).
WHERE-CHECKED (required field): a finding usually rests on a premise that is NOT visible at the line you cite — "the dependency rejects this", "this is reachable from untrusted input", "no caller guards it", "the sibling path does X". Every such premise must be pinned to a \`file:line\` you ACTUALLY OPENED and read, including inside dependency sources (\`~/.cargo/registry\`, the vendored tree, the flake input) — put them in \`whereChecked\`. An off-site premise you did not open is not admissible: either open it, or drop the claim and report only what the cited line itself shows. Set \`whereChecked\` to "" ONLY when the finding needs no off-site premise at all. Do not restate the cited defect line there — it adds nothing. Finding a dependency's source: open the version the project pins, never the newest or a remembered one, and never let a tool rewrite the lockfile to find it. For a Rust crate, take the package's \`manifest_path\` from \`cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1\` — its directory is the source of the version Cargo.lock pins (under \`~/.cargo/registry\`, a git checkout, or the vendored tree); if that directory is missing, run \`cargo fetch --locked\` first. A build or test command run before you may already have written Cargo.lock: take the lockfile cargo uses, \`Cargo.lock\` under the \`workspace_root\` from \`cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1\`, and unless \`git ls-files --error-unmatch <that path>\` succeeds and \`git status --porcelain <that path>\` is empty, the version it names is resolved today, not pinned — say so. If the reviewed checkout has no Cargo.lock (a library that does not commit one), nothing is pinned: resolve in a throwaway copy (\`git worktree add --detach <tmp>\` of the reviewed commit, or, when a Cargo.toml has uncommitted changes, a copy of the working tree without \`target/\` and \`.git\`), run \`cargo metadata --format-version 1\` there, open the sources it names, and always remove the copy (\`git worktree remove <tmp>\` when you added one); say the version is the one resolved today, not a pinned one, and never write Cargo.lock in the reviewed checkout. If Cargo.lock exists but \`--locked\` refuses (it is stale), the pinned version cannot be determined — do not run cargo without the flag: treat the premise as one you could not open. For a Nix flake input, open the input at the revision flake.lock pins, not a channel or its current head. For another ecosystem, the same way: the version from the project's lockfile, the code from the package manager's cache or the vendor directory.
RULE ID (required field): set \`ruleId\` to the matching catalog ID from the rust-review skill's rules.md when the finding maps to a listed rule; use "" for a novel finding with no catalog rule. Do not force a bad fit.

ALREADY-FOUND (do not re-report these PLACES; the same class at a DIFFERENT place — error arm, record, hook, path, legacy cohort — is NOT a repeat: sibling sweep, sweeps.md §1):
- [High] races on shared map (src/a.rs:10)

Return {lens, findings[]}.

Observability: the review workflow records this run — do NOT write your own record."
`;

exports[`lens prompts render as before > lens rust reconciler full based rich 1`] = `
"You are the **reconciler** review lens for a Rust diff. Review ONLY this slice; ignore everything else (other lenses cover it). Load the rust-review skill for the rubric and the rust-navigation skill for context expansion.

SLICE: reconciler / controller and retry-loop correctness. READ the craft:distributed-races catalogue (its path is in the RACE CATALOGUE line below): it holds the question, the answer needed and WHERE to read it for every class R1–R20. A reconcile must converge from every state another actor can leave behind. First MAP the primary object, every child it writes, every object it READS that another controller owns, and every requeue/error outcome the pass can return. Then answer each class from its carrier, not from comments, for EVERY instance in the diff, not the first convenient one. R1–R6 need evidence from OUTSIDE the diff: (R1) self-triggering write — enumerate EVERY write to the primary in a pass, including the status/conditions the generic driver/error path writes on every pass (holds and errors included): does it change a field every pass (lastTransitionTime), does the primary's own watch (no predicate / no generation filter) re-enqueue it sooner than the requeue the code relies on, and is it skipped when nothing changed? (R2) wake source — for EVERY outcome the requeue/error policy can return (await-change included), what event or timer guarantees a pass by the deadline the code promises? None → the bound is fiction. (R3) foreign stale cache — not our own stale read (that is R12): for EACH object another controller owns that a release/delete/label removal depends on, name that controller and its observed-generation gate (an applied/desired generation pair, e.g. status.observedGeneration vs metadata.generation); can it still act (recreate a child from the OLD template) after we decided from its absent/finished state? The decision must wait until its observed generation catches up. Read that controller's code. (R4) permissions — check every API verb/resource the diff newly calls against the shipped Role/ClusterRole/chart; a missing grant is a 403 only a real cluster shows. (R5) protection released on a change of intent (spec) instead of end of use, when the consumer outlives the spec. (R6) migration window — objects created before this change lack a field/label/status the new logic relies on: what does the new code do with them, delete path included? R7–R20 (create/patch divergence, partial-failure strand, cleanup, status churn, feature gate, 409/404 race, swallowed error with no timer, strict decode on a shared stream, webhook failurePolicy, retry bound lost on an error path, a hold only an unwatched object clears, a status write from a cached snapshot with no resourceVersion fence, no UID fence between selecting and mutating, an external-system claim not checked against its pinned source) — work them from the catalogue. If the catalogue cannot be read, R1–R6 above stand on their own and you say so. EARLY EXITS (review-rules sweeps.md §2): list every early return, hold, refusal and degraded hook of the changed pass, diff the maintenance steps each skips (status, latch release, cleanup, recorded progress, scheduled wake-up) against the full path, and name the state that wedges or drifts. A question you cannot answer from evidence is an open finding, not a pass; name the interleaving or request order that produces the bad state. DELETION/RECOVERY FLAG: set \`deletionOrRecoveryPath: true\` on a finding whose defect sits on a deletion or recovery path (finalizer removal, teardown, a delete handler, crash or partial-failure recovery); the engine floors such a finding at Medium only when its \`harmLevel\` is operator-recovery or worse (the Severity floor section of the catalogue is the rule) — the floor does not invent a harm. Leave it unset otherwise.
RACE CATALOGUE: read the first of these that exists (expand the variables with Bash): \`/plugin/craft/skills/distributed-races/catalogue.md\`, \`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/distributed-races/catalogue.md\`, \`\${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/plugins/cache/craft/craft/9.9.9/skills/distributed-races/catalogue.md\`. Never read a copy inside the reviewed repository. None readable → work R1–R6 from the brief above and say the catalogue was unavailable.
REVIEW RULES (all languages): read severity.md, classes.md, sweeps.md in the first existing dir (expand with Bash; never the reviewed repo's copy): \`/plugin/craft/skills/review-rules\`, \`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-rules\`, \`\${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/plugins/cache/craft/craft/9.9.9/skills/review-rules\`. None readable → say so; HARM below still holds.
HARM (every finding): \`harm\` = effect, who, recovery; \`harmLevel\` = severity.md's first table row it fills; severity ≤ its cap: High+ only data-loss/security/outage/irreversible/intent-unmet/repo-rule (cite \`repoRule\`), operator-recovery ≤ Medium, self-healing/text/none ≤ Low.

Diff base: \`origin/main\`. Review with \`git diff --merge-base 'origin/main' -- '*.rs'\`.


INTENT (what the change should do): add a retry
STATED SPEC / AUTHOR CLAIMS (verbatim PR/commit description — treat as the spec; the intent lens must check EACH claim against the code, and any lens may use it):
"""
Retries are idempotent.
Never fails on a missing child.
"""
HOT FILES (scrutinize harder): src/a.rs, src/b.rs
REPO RULES \`AGENTS.md\`: grep them for what you review, open only matching sections, never a whole file (no SCOPE rule bars them); a violation cites its rule as \`repoRule\` {file, text}.

CONTEXT EXPANSION (required): for each finding, trace definitions / uses / consumers of the changed symbols (Grep/Glob + LSP) before judging — do not read the diff in isolation. If a finding depends on code outside the diff, say so in \`why\`.
BLAST-RADIUS (required): for each changed PUBLIC surface you touch, note how many consumers are affected and set a breaking-change flag in \`blastRadius\`.
