- Another crate's behaviour matters to a finding (what it returns, what it guarantees, who else writes this state)? Do not read it — ASK.
BLOCKING QUESTION — only when you cannot go on without the answer: in one Bash call run \`id=u1-r2-$(date +%s%N); mkdir -p '/tmp/ch' && printf '%s' '<json>' > '/tmp/ch'/.q-$id.tmp && mv '/tmp/ch'/.q-$id.tmp '/tmp/ch'/q-$id.json; echo $id\` with <json> = {"from":"u1-r2","unit","symbol","path","ask","findingTitle","findingFile"} (a fresh id for every question, never reused). Then wait in ONE Bash call (Bash timeout 120000): \`for i in $(seq 20); do [ -e '/tmp/ch'/a-<id>.json ] && break; [ -e '/tmp/ch'/gone-r2 ] && break; sleep 5; done; cat '/tmp/ch'/a-<id>.json 2>/dev/null || { [ -e '/tmp/ch'/gone-r2 ] && echo NO-COORDINATOR || echo NO-ANSWER; }\`. NO-ANSWER → you may wait once more the same way; still none → treat it as unknown, answer "NO-ANSWER". NO-COORDINATOR → the coordinator has left: do not wait again, treat it as unknown, answer "NO-COORDINATOR".
- DEFERRED QUESTION — anything else: list it in \`questions\` and go on. List EVERY question you asked in \`questions\` (blocking: true if you wrote a q-file, answered: true if its answer came), naming the finding that depends on it in findingTitle and findingFile; a finding that rests on an unanswered question says so in \`why\`.
- whereChecked: premises inside \`crates/core/\` pinned to a file:line you opened; a premise about another crate is a question, not a whereChecked entry.
